  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 156 AM CDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nNegatively tilted trough coming up from the south brings in a plume \nof moisture from the Intermountain West. BUFKIT profiles are mainly \nmoist adiabatic, with the GEFS/EC PWATS right at about or a little \nshy of 2 standard deviations above climo.  Best forcing is centered \naround 600mb in an east-west axis that lifts north across the area \nduring the day. After that, a north-south oriented wave embedded \nwithin southwest flow aloft provides additional forcing. CAMS \nhighlight this area for stronger convection with this wave. There is \nan axis of instability but the GFS/NAM both have this axis  \nsouth of the CWA with limited elevated instability over us (NAM\npeaks at about 500j/kg MUCAPE). It comes down to the very high \nshear within this environment. 0-6km shear is about 50kts with \nREFS paintballs for 2-5km helicity and 10m wind speeds\nhighlighted in the latest SPC risk area. Thus, a high shear low\nCAPE setup. \n\nIn the wake of the system, with high pressure overhead and to \nthe northeast, light northeasterly flow and recent moisture \nmeans the potential for fog. CAMS are highlighting the upslope \nareas of the Sisseton hills.\n\nWe remain in a southwest flow regime in the wake of this system. \nThere is another southwest flow wave, albeit much weaker, for \nWednesday night. With such a weak system, NBM is only carrying about \na 20-40% chance for weather.  NAM BUFKIT profiles show skinny CAPE \nbut another favorable shear dynamic across the northeast. \n\nAn upper trough over the Pacific Northwest sits and deepens for the \nrest of the week and into the weekend, building/intensifying the \nridge aloft. 700mb temperatures are running at about a standard \ndeviation above climo for the latter half of week and through the \nweekend. The GFS/EC do bring a backdoor front/cooler low level \nairmass down into the region early next week.",
